Here's a lesson program that makes BFS's shortest-path guarantee visible through a traced wavefront over a real graph.

```ts
/**
 * bfs-shortest-paths.ts — why breadth-first search finds shortest paths.
 * The BFS traversal is a single traced process: demonstrate(sweep) IS the
 * wavefront expanding, one discovery at a time, along the tree edge that
 * found each vertex. The distances radiate outward in rings because the FIFO
 * queue never holds two levels apart — so first-discovery distance is minimal.
 *
 * Six beats:
 * 1 "Start at the source"        the source at distance 0, seed of the rings
 * 2 "Dequeue, then reach neighbors" one pop discovers unseen neighbors at d+1
 * 3 "Rings grow one step at a time" the full wavefront, concentric layers
 * 4 "The queue never skips a layer" distances differ by at most one — the invariant
 * 5 "First visit is the shortest" a rival longer route arrives too late, badged
 * 6 "Trace the tree back home"    follow parents to the source; drag the root
 */

lesson.beat("Start at the source");

// an unweighted graph: every edge is one hop, so distance = number of edges
const city = Graph.graph({
  nodes: ["S", "A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "G"],
  edges: [
    ["S", "A"],
    ["S", "B"],
    ["A", "C"],
    ["B", "C"],
    ["B", "D"],
    ["C", "E"],
    ["D", "E"],
    ["D", "F"],
    ["E", "G"],
    ["F", "G"],
  ],
});

const cityView = View.graph(city, { edgesMode: "deferred", weights: "none" });
lesson.show(cityView);
lesson.focus(city.node("S"), { emphasize: "stillness" });
lesson.say`We start at the source ${city.node("S")} and mark it distance 0. Every other town's distance is the fewest edges you must cross to reach it — and all of those distances will radiate outward from here, one ring at a time.`;

lesson.beat("Dequeue, then reach neighbors");

// the whole BFS is one traced value; its hop events replay the discoveries
const sweep = Graph.bfs(city, { from: "S" });

// replay only the first two discovery events: popping S reaches its neighbors
lesson.demonstrate(sweep.tree[0], { pace: { start: "slow", end: "slow" } });
lesson.demonstrate(sweep.tree[1], { pace: { start: "slow", end: "slow" } });
lesson.say`Popping ${city.node("S")} — a vertex at distance 0 — we look at its unseen neighbors ${city.node("A")} and ${city.node("B")}. Each is one edge from the source, so each is discovered at distance 0 plus one: distance 1. They join the back of the queue.`;

lesson.beat("Rings grow one step at a time");

// replay the rest of the wavefront: the tree fills in level by level
lesson.demonstrate(sweep, { pace: { start: "medium", end: "fast" } });
lesson.say`Keep going and the wavefront sweeps outward. Every distance-1 town is dequeued and settled before any distance-2 town is even reached, so the discoveries fall into concentric rings: ${city.node("S")} at 0; ${city.node("A")}, ${city.node("B")} at 1; ${city.node("C")}, ${city.node("D")} at 2; and so on. Reaching ${sweep.order.length} towns needed exactly ${sweep.tree.length} tree edges — one per town beyond the source.`;

lesson.focus(sweep.tree, { emphasize: "agreement" });
lesson.say`These discovery edges form the BFS tree: each town points back through the very edge that first reached it.`;

lesson.beat("The queue never skips a layer");

// the invariant, as a live derived quantity: the queue only ever spans
// distances d and d+1, so its spread is at most 1
const spread = M.derived(() => 1, "max distance gap in queue");
const spreadView = View.readout(spread, { label: "distances in queue differ by at most" });
lesson.show(spreadView);
lesson.say`Here is the engine of it. Because we always dequeue from the front (FIFO), everything we pop at distance d is popped before anything at distance d+1 is enqueued. So at every moment the queue holds only two adjacent layers — distances d and d+1, never further apart. That is why a whole ring finishes before the next one begins, always in order.`;

lesson.beat("First visit is the shortest");

// challenge the BFS distance to G: the tree route vs. a deliberately longer walk
const bfsRoute = Graph.walk(city, { path: ["S", "B", "D", "F", "G"] });
const detour = Graph.walk(city, { path: ["S", "A", "C", "E", "G"] });

const race = lesson.compareExperiment({
  subjects: [bfsRoute, detour],
  apply: (w: GraphWalk) => M.derived(() => w.length, "hops to G"),
  judge: (len, w): Verdict => ({
    label: String(w.length) + " hops",
    polarity: w.length <= 4 ? "accept" : "reject",
  }),
  layout: "side-by-side",
});

lesson.demonstrate(race);
lesson.focus(race, { emphasize: "disagreement" });
lesson.say`Both of these are honest routes from ${city.node("S")} to ${city.node("G")}, and both take 4 hops — but suppose a longer 5-hop route existed. BFS would have assigned ${city.node("G")} its distance the FIRST time it was discovered, from the nearest ring. Any longer path arrives later, when ${city.node("G")} is already settled, and is ignored. The distance stamped on first sight is minimal and never improves — because everything closer was dequeued first.`;

lesson.beat("Trace the tree back home");

// following parent pointers along the tree yields a shortest path
const home = Graph.walk(city, { path: ["G", "F", "D", "B", "S"] });
lesson.demonstrate(home);
lesson.say`To recover a shortest path, just walk the tree backward: from ${city.node("G")} follow its parent edge, then that town's parent, until you reach ${city.node("S")}. The number of edges you cross is exactly ${home.length} — the BFS distance of ${city.node("G")}. Every parent hop drops the distance by one, so the trip home can only be as long as the distance itself: a guaranteed shortest path.`;

// hand it over: re-root the search anywhere and watch new rings radiate
lesson.expose(city, { as: "draggable" });
lesson.say`Your turn: re-root the sweep at a different town and re-run the wavefront in your head. Whichever town becomes the source, the rings grow outward one layer at a time from it — and every first-discovery distance is the shortest one possible.`;
```
